<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aman Andom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aman Andom was an Ethiopian general of Eritrean origin who became the first chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, the military committee that deposed Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, in September 1974. He opposed a military solution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and the execution of former officials, and was killed resisting arrest on 23 November 1974, the night on which the Derg executed 59 prisoners.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aman-andom.jpg" alt="Aman Andom" width="220" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aman_andom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aman Mikael Andom was born on 21 June 1924 in the village of Tsazega in Italian Eritrea, one of five children, and was a Lutheran. Educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> with the British forces that defeated the Italians and restored Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> to the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He commanded Ethiopian contingents in the Korean War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-crisis">Congo Crisis</a> and was promoted to major general in 1962. During the Ethiopian–Somali Border War of 1964 he earned the nickname Desert Lion after a battle with Somali forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>. There is some evidence that he was in contact with the officers of the junta as early as February and March 1974, and by July he had been appointed its chief of staff.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 15 September 1974, three days after the emperor was taken from his palace to imprisonment at the headquarters of the Fourth Division, the officers made Aman their chairman and acting head of state, while proclaiming Crown Prince Asfaw Wossen king-designate. According to the historians Ottaway, he was at odds with most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> from the first day, even over whether he was its chairman or only its spokesman. He thought the council too large, wanted a negotiated settlement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a> where his opponents sought to crush it, and refused to approve the execution of former high officials, among them two former prime ministers and members of the royal family.</p><p>He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> twice, from 25 August to 6 September and again in November, promising an end to old practices and an inquiry into the army's crimes against Eritreans. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> soldiers stormed the camp of the Corps of Engineers on 7 October, he sought support in the wider army, and on 15 November he sent all units a message highly critical of the Derg. Two days later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengistu-haile-mariam">Mengistu Haile Mariam</a> demanded 5,000 men for Eritrea and the execution of six imprisoned officials.</p><p>Aman refused, resigned and retired to his house, appealing secretly to the Third Division, but Mengistu intercepted the appeals. He was killed in a gunfight with the troops sent to arrest him on 23 November 1974; the historian Bahru Zewde concludes that he had become an obstacle to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>'s exercise of power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aman Andom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…15 September 1974, the Derg took full control of government under a unitary form and chose Lieutenant General <b>Aman Andom</b> as chairman and head of state, pending the return of Crown Prince Asfaw Wossen as a constitutional monarch; when Aman quarrelled with the radicals over Eritrea and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tsazega, in what is now <a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.35,38.8&amp;year=1956" aria-label="Tsazega on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5609 1593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aman Andom"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6059 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6059 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.35,38.8&amp;year=1956">What was here in 1956? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
